// ==========================================================================
// data/planes/cessna172/geometry.jsonc for the Cessna 172 source analysis, generated -- do not hand-edit.
//
// 1. SOLVER, MESH, FLIGHT CONDITION
//    Solver: Tornado, via the sibling package aid.tornado (imported from ../../../../../../mnt/VirtualMachine/FlightSimulation/USAF_DATCOM/AircraftIntuitiveDesign/Python/src,
//    a path relative to THIS repository's root so the bytes do not depend on which
//    mount point the repo is reached through; see provenance.aid_src.
//    Sibling commit: b6921558abfce291a029a0809b283c7978da2d3c.  It is recorded
//    because an upstream commit has broken this converter twice, silently both times -- a rebuild
//    that moves it should be a one-line diff in this field and nothing else.
//    Mesh: 10 chordwise x 5 spanwise (the AID batch default).  NP[0],
//    the AR 33.3, S 3 ft^2 panel, is KEPT in the lattice and carries
//    9.6% of the aircraft's CL at the trim alpha.
//    Flight condition from the source AERO: Mach 0.03, altitude 0.0 ft, betha 0.0 rad,
//    evaluated at alpha = 4 deg (the middle of AERO.ALSCHD), V = 3.1112 m/s,
//    rho = 1.225056 kg/m^3.  Tornado's state["AS"] is in FT/S -- it is 10.2073 ft/s, and AID works in feet
//    throughout (aid.atmosphere.atmosphere(0)['a'] = 1116.288876590643 ft/s) -- so the m/s figure above
//    is that value x 0.3048, and both are recorded in flight_condition.  This is the SOURCE Mach-0.03 speed;
//    the trimmed cruise speed the model actually flies at is in trim.cruise_mps.
//
// 2. THE SOURCE PLANFORM IS A CESSNA 172 AT ROUGHLY 1/3 LINEAR SCALE
//    span 12 ft against a real 36.1 ft, chord 2 ft against 4.89 ft, area 24 ft^2
//    against 174 ft^2.  Wing loading is therefore 6.67 lbf/ft^2 where a real C172 is
//    13.2.  Nothing in this file may be compared with a full-scale figure.
//
// 3. MASS
//    mass_kg = 72.574 is derived from the source's own AERO.WT = 5 slugs:
//    floor(AERO.WT * 32.0 ft/s^2 / 2.2046226 lbf/kg * 1000)/1000 = floor(5 * 32.0 / 2.2046226 * 1000)/1000 = 72.574 kg, truncated from 72.5747799 kg,
//    i.e. 160 lbf.  A slug is a mass unit, so the source's WT converts instead as
//    AERO.WT * SLUG_TO_KG = 72.96951 kg; the plan's figure assumes g = 32.0 ft/s^2, which
//    appears nowhere in the source (its own G_FT_S2 is 32.174), so the plan is 0.54 % low and everything derived from the weight is too.
//    This is NOT a geometrically similar 1/3 scale C172,
//    which would weigh about 53.5 kg, so no scaling of a published full-scale number is self-
//    consistent and none was attempted.  ixx, iyy, izz come from radius-of-gyration rules on THIS
//    mass and THIS span (see below); ixz = 0 and he = 0.
//
// 4. UNIT CONVERSIONS APPLIED, AND THE ONES NOT TRUSTED
//    length: ft x 0.3048 = 0.3048 m; angle: degrees x 57.29577951308232 (= DEG_TO_RAD) per radian.
//    aid.handbook_pass._per_rad / _per_deg were NOT used: they are heuristic
//    (`if a > 1: convert else pass`), _per_rad is a no-op below 1.0, and on this
//    aircraft that silently makes a DATCOM rate derivative 57x too small.  Every
//    per-degree number here is multiplied by 57.29577951308232 explicitly.
//    Every array in this file is per radian and every length is SI.
//    cx[0] -- the parasite drag slot, Cd0 in a drag build-up -- = -0.017219.
//    It is NOT a Tornado output.  Source: source WG.CD0 (the plan's formula, Tornado CD(0) - K_wing*CL(0)^2 = -0.001673, is negative: a linear VLM has no profile drag).
//    CD(0) = 0.000603563 and K*CL(0)^2 = 0.00227614 with K = 0.0803732,
//    so CD(0) - K*CL(0)^2 is negative: a linear vortex-lattice run has no profile
//    drag at all.  The source file's own WG.CD0 = 0.017219 is used instead.
//
// 5. THE MOMENT REFERENCE SHIFT
//    Tornado reports about geo['ref_point'] = [0, 0, 0] while geo['CG'] = [2.94, 0, 0] ft, so the
//    reference point is 2.94 ft AHEAD of the CG in the x-FORWARD sense the sibling returns.
//    The offset handed to shift_moments_to_cg is dx = -ft(2.94) = -0.896112 m (NOT +), with dy = dz = 0.
//    The shift's cz is the BODY-Z force coefficient, and coeff_create's CZ IS that
//    coefficient, already in plane/dynamics.py's frame, so it is used as it stands:
//    slopes shift with slopes (Cm_a with CZ_a, Cm_Q with CZ_Q, Cm_de with CZ_de
//    reconstructed exactly from the control row's wind-axis CL and CD) and the Cm0
//    intercept shifts with the body-z intercept CZ(0), which at alpha = 0 is the same
//    number as the -CL(0) that gives cz[0] its sign.  CL_a and CZ_a differ by 0.34 % here because
//    NP[0] is in the lattice.  The yaw slopes shift with the BODY-Y side force (Cn_beta
//    with CY_b, Cn_p with CY_P, Cn_r with CY_R).  No drag coefficient enters any shift: cx reaches
//    the pitch line only through dz and the yaw line only through dy, and both arms are zero
//    for this aircraft.
//
// 6. SIGN CONVENTION
//    cz is the body +z force coefficient with z DOWN, so cz = -CL and cz[1] is NEGATIVE for a
//    stable aircraft (CL_alpha = -cz[1] > 0), and cm[1] is negative too.  That identity is
//    load-bearing; it is what makes data/planes/linear/morelli.json's cz[1] = -4.5 CORRECT, not a
//    defect of that file.
//    data/planes/linear/morelli.json is the sign-convention authority wherever it disagrees with
//    data/planes/f16/morelli.json.  It WINS on cm[1] (-0.8 against +0.0466) and on cnp[0] (-0.03
//    against +0.0268), where a positive value would be statically unstable.  It LOSES on cnda[0]
//    (+0.01 against -0.0335), where adverse yaw fixes the sign.  A reader must not assume the two
//    sets share a convention: this file and linear/morelli.json agree on cz[1] and disagree with
//    the F-16 set on cm[1] and cnp[0].
//
//    NO FRAME CONVERSION IS APPLIED IN THIS FILE, and that is the new normal.
//    aid/axes.py is the sibling's single home for solver signs and
//    aid/tornado/coeff.py's last statement is `return to_frd("tornado", out)`, so
//    everything read here is ALREADY x forward, y right, z DOWN with positive Cl
//    rolling right, positive Cm nose up and positive Cn yawing right --
//    plane/dynamics.py's own frame and senses.  The control rows are already
//    Forward-Right-Down too, and aid/tornado/control_deriv.py says on purpose that it
//    does not re-wrap them.  The earlier mapping `cz = -CL; cl = -Cl; cm = +Cm;
//    cn = -Cn; cy = +CY` existed only to convert Tornado's former aft-positive x and
//    up-positive z, and it is GONE: cl, cm, cn and cy are read exactly as reported,
//    cx[0] carries the negated source parasite magnitude (item 4) rather than a solver
//    number, and the P and R columns need no flip because they no longer arrive inverted.
//
//    3 negations survive, and none is a frame conversion.  Each is a SIGN-CONVENTION identity that the schema
//    itself requires -- `cz = -CL` on cz[1], cz[0] and cz[5] -- its body +z axis points down, not a change
//    of axis.  Listed from tornado.jsonc's provenance.mapping:
//      cz[1] from CL_a = 5.148013 -> -5.148013 per radian
//      cz[0] from CL(alpha=0) = 0.168285 -> -0.168285
//      cz[5] from tornado_controls[elevator].CL = 0.006480 per degree -> -0.371259 per radian
//
//    Everything else is unnegated, including czq[0] = CZ_Q and cxq[0] = CX_Q, which are
//    the body-axis coefficients the schema actually adds to cz and cx.
//
//    Signs are normalised mechanically against aero_convert.units.SIGN_INVARIANTS and
//    every flip is logged in provenance.flipped.  Computed from this run's own flipped
//    list rather than written out, it is ALL of them:
//      ['cd_q', 'cl_dr', 'cn_da', 'cn_dr', 'cn_p', 'cy_da', 'cy_dr']
//    of which the rate and sideslip columns -- the ones this header used to print on
//    their own, under words that claimed they were the whole list -- are:
//      ['cd_q', 'cn_p']
//    Those are convention differences, not frame errors: the invariant table is the
//    final authority, and each is a slot whose SIGN the two in-tree reference files
//    disagree on.  cm0 carries no invariant and is recorded as not-normalised.
//
//    A CAVEAT on the 9 genuinely per-phat / per-qhat / per-rhat slots:
//    cyp[0], cyr[0], czq[0], cmq[0], clp[0], clr[0], cnp[0], cnr[0], cxq[0].
//    cy[1] and cy[2] are NOT in that list: they are cy_da and cy_dr, the
//    aileron and rudder side force per radian of CONTROL DEFLECTION, which no
//    flight-condition speed touches.  Inherited from the flight condition and
//    NOT corrected here.
//    coeff_create quotes them per p-hat = p*b_ref/(2*AS) using the SOURCE speed
//    state["AS"] = 10.2073 ft/s = 3.1112 m/s (Mach 0.03, altitude 0 ft), while
//    f16/aero_morelli.py forms phat = p*b_m/(2*V) with V the model's own speed,
//    23.1146 m/s at the trim over the same 3.6576 m span.  Both scalings are
//    internally consistent -- Tornado works in feet and ft/s throughout, and the sound speed it uses is in
//    ft/s too -- so this is NOT a unit error inside Tornado; it is a flight-condition mismatch, and
//    its effect is that dp-hat/dP is 0.58781 in Tornado against
//    0.07912 in the model, so the model's p-hat scaling is 7.43x weaker.  The written rate
//    derivatives are therefore 7.43x smaller than the derivative with respect to the
//    model's own phat, i.e. the model's rate-damping terms are that much weaker than the solver's
//    numbers imply at the trimmed speed, and CL_Q = 9.591 is not a textbook per-q-hat
//    value; the pitch-rate slot's own two numbers are printed from tornado.jsonc's provenance.cd_q_convention
//    immediately below.  NOT rescaled here: the plan fixes the flight condition from the source AERO,
//    and rescaling it is a physics decision for a later task.
//
//    THE PITCH-RATE SLOT IS BODY-AXIS, not standard-aero.  Written out from
//    tornado.jsonc's provenance.cd_q_convention so it cannot drift from what shipped:
//      shipped:      czq[0] = coeff_create['CZ_Q'] and cxq[0] = coeff_create['CX_Q'], the BODY-axis pitch-rate coefficients, read unnegated
//      why:          the schema ADDS both slots to the body-axis cz and cx, so the body-axis coefficient is what they mean.  The sibling returns both in Forward-Right-Down, so no identity and no conversion apply
//      czq[0] writes -9.615046 (CZ_Q, body axis)
//      the WIND-axis CL_Q is 9.591131, i.e. -9.591131 in this slot if standard-aero
//      convention were used -- it is NOT what this file writes.  The two differ by 0.249 per cent,
//      because CL and CZ part company wherever the axial force is non-zero.
//      Any sentence in this header quoting a wind-axis CL_Q number means the
//      WIND-axis one above, not the value in the arrays.
//
// 7. PER-SLOT PROVENANCE (all 19 arrays; real solver output vs zeroed)
//   cz[1]     cl_alpha   normalised     solver CL_a = 5.14801 -> mapped -5.14801 -> written -5.14801
//   cz[0]     cl0        normalised     solver CL(alpha=0) = 0.168285 -> mapped -0.168285 -> written -0.168285
//   czq[0]    cl_q       normalised     solver CZ_Q = -9.61505 -> mapped -9.61505 -> written -9.61505
//   cy[0]     cy_beta    normalised     solver CY_b = -0.40827 -> mapped -0.40827 -> written -0.40827
//   cyp[0]    cy_p       normalised     solver CY_P = -0.159618 -> mapped -0.159618 -> written -0.159618
//   cyr[0]    cy_r       normalised     solver CY_R = 0.386951 -> mapped 0.386951 -> written 0.386951
//   cl[0]     cl_beta    normalised     solver Cl_b = -0.0537212 -> mapped -0.0537212 -> written -0.0537212
//   clp[0]    cl_p       normalised     solver Cl_P = -0.486095 -> mapped -0.486095 -> written -0.486095
//   clr[0]    cl_r       normalised     solver Cl_R = 0.044525 -> mapped 0.044525 -> written 0.044525
//   cm[1]     cm_alpha   normalised     solver Cm_a = -8.78614 -> mapped -1.19274 -> written -1.19274
//   cm[0]     cm0        not-normalised solver Cm(alpha=0) = -0.231932 -> mapped 0.0154461 -> written 0.0154461
//   cmq[0]    cm_q       normalised     solver Cm_Q = -33.3488 -> mapped -19.2147 -> written -19.2147
//   cn[0]     cn_beta    normalised     solver Cn_b = 0.2465 -> mapped 0.146473 -> written 0.146473
//   cnp[0]    cn_p       flipped        solver Cn_P = 0.0686714 -> mapped 0.0295649 -> written -0.0295649
//   cnr[0]    cn_r       normalised     solver Cn_R = -0.286403 -> mapped -0.1916 -> written -0.1916
//   cxq[0]    cd_q       flipped        solver CX_Q = -0.00707928 -> mapped -0.00707928 -> written 0.00707928
//   cx[0]     cd0        normalised     NOT solver output -- cd0 <- source WG.CD0 (the plan's formula, Tornado CD(0) - K_wing*CL(0)^2 = -0.001673, is negative: a linear VLM has no profile drag) -> mapped -0.017219 -> written -0.017219
//   clda[0]   cl_da      normalised     solver tornado_controls[aileron].Cl = -0.00428109 -> mapped -0.245288 -> written -0.245288
//   cy[1]     cy_da      flipped        solver tornado_controls[aileron].CY = -0.000875673 -> mapped -0.0501724 -> written 0.0501724
//   cnda[0]   cn_da      flipped        solver tornado_controls[aileron].Cn = 0.000311384 -> mapped 0.017841 -> written -0.017841
//   cldr[0]   cl_dr      flipped        solver tornado_controls[rudder].Cl = -0.000374454 -> mapped -0.0214546 -> written 0.0214546
//   cy[2]     cy_dr      flipped        solver tornado_controls[rudder].CY = -0.00400424 -> mapped -0.229426 -> written 0.229426
//   cndr[0]   cn_dr      flipped        solver tornado_controls[rudder].Cn = 0.00319877 -> mapped 0.183276 -> written -0.183276
//   cz[5]     cl_de      normalised     solver tornado_controls[elevator].CL = 0.00647969 -> mapped -0.371259 -> written -0.371259
//   cm[2]     cm_de      normalised     solver tornado_controls[elevator].Cm = -0.0298079 -> mapped -1.16154 -> written -1.16154
//      cx[1] (CD_alpha) and cx[3] (CD_de) are real schema slots: LINEAR_INDEX
//      lists cx's linear indexes as (0, 1, 3) and f16/aero_morelli.py
//      evaluates Cx0 = cx[0] + cx[1]*alpha + ... + cx[3]*de, so the flight
//      model reads both terms. They ship 0.0 because DerivativeSet has no
//      field for either derivative and aero_convert/morelli.py's SLOT_MAP
//      maps only ('cx', ((0, 'cd0'),)) -- a MAPPING gap, not a schema gap.
//      The linear slots this file leaves at 0.0 without a provenance.missing
//      entry are: cx[1] and cx[3]
//    tornado.jsonc's provenance.zeroed_slots.CD_alpha carries the same fact in full.
//
// 8. HOW TO REGENERATE THIS FILE
//    cd python && python3 scripts/build_cessna172_aero.py
//    Nothing else writes these files; the sibling analysis and the sibling aid tree
//    are read-only inputs and all solver scratch goes to a temporary directory.
//
// 9. THE OTHER THREE SOLVERS' RAW OUTPUT
//    This file also carries `raw_cross_checks`, keyed by model, because a model file's
//    provenance.mapping holds only the ONE raw number behind each slot.  Without this block
//    the rest of each solver's output would not be re-derivable from the committed
//    tree without re-running the solver:
//      datcom  raw_cross_checks.datcom: 30 keys -- aid_CL0_as_reported, aid_CL_level_flight, aid_CLa_as_reported, aid_Cm0_as_reported, aid_Cma_as_reported, aid_K, aid_drag_CD0, aid_lateral_as_reported, aid_longitudinal_dynamic_CLq, aid_x_cg, datcom_alt, datcom_cd, datcom_cl, datcom_cla_per_degree, datcom_clb_per_degree, datcom_cm, datcom_cma_per_degree, datcom_cn, datcom_cnb_per_degree, datcom_cyb_per_degree, datcom_mach, datcom_table_alpha_deg, handbook_controls_per_degree, lateral_corrected, lateral_dynamic_as_reported, planform, section7, solver_alpha_deg, solver_alpha_index, solver_alpha_schedule_deg
//      avl     raw_cross_checks.avl: 12 keys -- alpha_deg, alpha_index, alpha_schedule_deg, avl_axes, control_rows_per_degree, control_surfaces_available, mesh, planform, reference_case, sb_geometry_axis, st_alpha_rows, x_reference_ft
//      flow5   raw_cross_checks.flow5: 24 keys -- CD, CDind, CDvis, CL, CLa, Cm, Cma, Cx, Cz, alpha_deg, alpha_index, alpha_schedule_deg, beta_flat, cog_m, control_row_frame, control_rows_per_degree, deck_polar, deck_wings, entry_point, frame, mesh, output_keys, planform, stab_derivatives
//    Every per-degree number in them is UNCONVERTED: the model files carry the
//    x 57.29577951308232 step and the sign normalisation, those blocks do not.
//    flow5 has none -- its deck and polar are already SI and per radian -- which is
//    itself part of that solver's record.
// ==========================================================================
